<h2>The clause in question</h2>
<p>Ahluwalia Contracts issued a work order of about Rs 1.99 crore to Janvee Contracts in May 2021 for concrete mixing, transportation and pumping at a medical college project in Hamirpur. When Janvee raised claims of about Rs 19.30 lakh — including a security deposit of Rs 4.61 lakh — and invoked arbitration by a Section 21 notice, Ahluwalia replied that there was no arbitration agreement at all.</p>
<p>Clause 18 of the work order's Annexure-III, headed "Arbitration and Resolution of Dispute", said disputes would be "resolved amicably in consultation with Chairman-ACIL", whose decision would be final and binding. Clause 19 gave the courts of Delhi exclusive jurisdiction. The only issue before the Court: is Clause 18 an arbitration agreement under Section 7, so that an arbitrator can be appointed under Section 11(6)?</p>

<h2>The Court's analysis</h2>
<p>At the Section 11 stage the examination is confined to the prima facie existence of an arbitration agreement, per <em>SBI General Insurance v. Krish Spinning</em>, (2024) 12 SCC 1. Working from the classic attributes in <em>K.K. Modi v. K.N. Modi</em>, (1998) 3 SCC 573, and <em>Jagdish Chander v. Ramesh Chander</em>, (2007) 5 SCC 719, the Court found Clause 18 missing every marker of adjudication:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Consultation, not adjudication.</strong> The operative words provide for amicable resolution in consultation with the Chairman — no reference of disputes to him for decision, no submission of claims and defences, no procedure of hearing or evidence, no decision according to law.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No arbitral fallback.</strong> The clause contains no step referring disputes to arbitration if the amicable attempt fails.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>An internal mechanism.</strong> The Chairman is an officer of one contracting party, which marks the process as internal rather than neutral adjudication.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Labels do not control.</strong> Neither the heading nor "final and binding" language converts consultation into arbitration — reinforced by <em>Alchemist Hospitals v. ICT Health Technology</em>, 2025 INSC 1289, where even repeated use of the word "arbitration" did not save the clause.</div>
</div>
<p>Reading Clauses 18 and 19 together sealed the conclusion: parties who had chosen the exclusive jurisdiction of Delhi courts for their disputes had not, in substance, agreed to arbitrate. The petition was dismissed, with liberty to pursue remedies in accordance with law and no observation on the merits.</p>

<h2>Drafting lessons</h2>
<p>This genre of litigation is almost entirely preventable at the drafting table. A workable arbitration clause does four things in one sentence: refers all disputes arising out of or in connection with the contract to arbitration; fixes the number and mode of appointment of arbitrators (or designates an institution such as DIAC); states the seat; and declares the award binding. Conversely, contractors reviewing standard-form work orders should flag "amicable resolution" clauses dressed in arbitral vocabulary — they leave the subcontractor with no tribunal and, as here, a dismissed petition a year after the Section 21 notice.</p>
